3  ON REAL DATA -- and DOES A FIXED 72-BAR WINDOW STRADDLE A BOUNDARY? (the principal's point)
====================================================================================================

   root   sess   segs/sess   % sessions   median   chop share   trend share   C(72) from
                              >1 segment   seg len   of bars      of bars       own returns
     ES    800    1.00          0.1%         72      53.5%        46.5%        0.987
     NQ    800    1.01          0.8%         72      57.6%        42.4%        1.065
     GC    800    1.00          0.0%         72      68.4%        31.6%        1.212
     ZN    800    1.01          0.8%         72      53.8%        46.2%        1.014
     CL    800    1.02          1.8%         72      57.7%        42.3%        1.179

   READ THE '>1 SEGMENT' COLUMN. That is the fraction of sessions where ONE 72-bar number
   provably reports a BLEND of two or more regimes -- the principal's objection, measured.
   And the C(72) column is why the benchmark is re-estimated per root: it is not 1.000, and
   it is not even the Gaussian value.
